msgstr "![An image showing that each computing node might include many types of resources.](/learning/images/courses/integrating-quantum-and-high-performance-computing/compute-resources/resources.avif)\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Scaling classical resources {#scaling-classical-resources}"
msgstr "Menskalakan sumber klasik {#scaling-classical-resources}"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "High-Performance Computing (HPC) involves scaling these classical resources to achieve faster processing times or increase the data that can be simultaneously handled (for example, to increase the size of solutions spaces that can be searched). This can be achieved through:\n"
msgstr "Pengiraan Berprestasi Tinggi (HPC) melibatkan penskalaan sumber klasik ini untuk mencapai masa pemprosesan yang lebih pantas atau meningkatkan data yang boleh dikendalikan secara serentak (contohnya, untuk meningkatkan saiz ruang penyelesaian yang boleh dicari). Ini boleh dicapai melalui:\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, no-wrap
msgid "Vertical scaling: Increasing the power of individual resources, such as using a more powerful CPU or adding more memory within one physical node, where a node is a unit of a compute cluster encapsulating multiple computing resources within it.\n"
msgstr "Penskalaan menegak: Meningkatkan kuasa sumber individu, seperti menggunakan CPU yang lebih berkuasa atau menambah lebih banyak ingatan dalam satu nod fizikal, di mana nod ialah unit kluster pengiraan yang merangkumi pelbagai sumber pengiraan di dalamnya.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, no-wrap
msgid "Horizontal scaling: Adding more resources, such as multiple CPUs or GPUs, to work together on a single node or, more commonly, on multiple nodes, enabling distributed computation.\n"
msgstr "Penskalaan mendatar: Menambah lebih banyak sumber, seperti berbilang CPU atau GPU, untuk bekerjasama dalam satu nod atau, lebih lazimnya, pada berbilang nod, membolehkan pengiraan teragih.\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![An image showing vertical scaling of resources through placing more resources, like memory, within a single node, and horizontal scaling through increasing the number of connected nodes including different resource types.](/learning/images/courses/integrating-quantum-and-high-performance-computing/compute-resources/resource-scaling.avif)\n"
msgstr "![An image showing vertical scaling of resources through placing more resources, like memory, within a single node, and horizontal scaling through increasing the number of connected nodes including different resource types.](/learning/images/courses/integrating-quantum-and-high-performance-computing/compute-resources/resource-scaling.avif)\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Some of the scaling concepts from this section will be applicable to the next section on quantum computing resources. Some other aspects of quantum resources will be quantified in new ways.\n"
msgstr "Beberapa konsep penskalaan daripada bahagian ini akan terpakai pada bahagian seterusnya mengenai sumber pengiraan kuantum. Beberapa aspek lain sumber kuantum akan dikuantifikasikan dengan cara baharu.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Check your understanding {#check-your-understanding}"
msgstr "Uji kefahaman anda {#check-your-understanding}"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Use the descriptions above to infer some advantages and disadvantages of the different scaling approaches (vertical and horizontal).\n"
msgstr "Gunakan penerangan di atas untuk membuat kesimpulan tentang beberapa kelebihan dan kekurangan pendekatan penskalaan yang berbeza: menegak dan mendatar?\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Accordion>\n"
"<AccordionItem title=\"Answer\">\n"
msgstr ""
"<Accordion>\n"
"<AccordionItem title=\"Jawapan\">\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "There might be many correct answers. Vertical scaling is often simpler, especially if you have predictable workloads that will need a fixed amount of resources. But vertical scaling could be more expensive to upgrade, since the fundamental unit of computing cannot be broken down as easily as in horizontal scaling. Horizontal scaling is more complex to manage and sometimes there are difficulties or latencies related to connections between nodes. But it is much more adaptive to varying resource requirements and is modular when upgrades are required.\n"
msgstr "Mungkin ada banyak jawapan yang betul. Penskalaan menegak sering lebih mudah, terutamanya jika anda mempunyai beban kerja yang boleh diramal dan memerlukan jumlah sumber yang tetap. Tetapi penskalaan menegak boleh lebih mahal untuk dinaik taraf, kerana unit asas pengiraan tidak boleh dipecahkan dengan mudah seperti dalam penskalaan mendatar. Penskalaan mendatar lebih kompleks untuk diuruskan dan kadangkala terdapat kesukaran atau kependaman berkaitan sambungan antara nod. Tetapi ia jauh lebih mudah menyesuaikan diri dengan keperluan sumber yang berbeza-beza dan bersifat modular apabila naik taraf diperlukan.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"</AccordionItem>\n"
"</Accordion>\n"
msgstr ""
"</AccordionItem>\n"
"</Accordion>\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Title ##
#, no-wrap
msgid "New type of resource: QPU (Quantum Processing Unit) {#new-type-of-resource-qpu-quantum-processing-unit}"
msgstr "Jenis sumber baharu: QPU (Unit Pemprosesan Kuantum) {#new-type-of-resource-qpu-quantum-processing-unit}"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "In this section, we will introduce a new type of resource - a quantum resource - and explore its definition, units of measure, and connectivity to classical infrastructure.\n"
msgstr "Dalam bahagian ini, kita akan memperkenalkan jenis sumber baharu — sumber kuantum — dan meneroka definisi, unit ukuran, dan sambungannya ke infrastruktur klasik.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Definition of QPU {#definition-of-qpu}"
msgstr "Definisi QPU {#definition-of-qpu}"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, no-wrap
msgid "Quantum processing unit (QPU): A QPU includes all of the hardware responsible for accepting an executable quantum instruction set, or a quantum circuit, and returning an accurate answer.\n"
msgstr "Unit pemprosesan kuantum (QPU): QPU merangkumi semua perkakasan yang bertanggungjawab untuk menerima set arahan kuantum yang boleh dilaksanakan, atau litar kuantum, dan mengembalikan jawapan yang tepat.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "That means the QPU includes one or more quantum chips (e.g. Heron), the multiple additional components in the dilution refrigerator such as the quantum amplifiers, the control electronics, and the classical compute required for tasks such as holding the instructions and waveforms in memory, accumulating results, and future error correction decoding. While a dilution refrigerator is required to perform these tasks, we exclude the dilution refrigerator from this definition to allow for the case of multiple QPUs in the same fridge.\n"
msgstr "Ini bermakna QPU merangkumi satu atau lebih cip kuantum (contohnya Heron), pelbagai komponen tambahan dalam penyejuk pencairan seperti penguat kuantum, elektronik kawalan, dan pengiraan klasik yang diperlukan untuk tugas seperti menyimpan arahan dan bentuk gelombang dalam ingatan, mengumpulkan keputusan, dan penyahkodan pembetulan ralat masa hadapan. Walaupun penyejuk pencairan diperlukan untuk melaksanakan tugas-tugas ini, kita tidak memasukkan penyejuk pencairan dalam definisi ini bagi membolehkan kes berbilang QPU dalam peti sejuk yang sama.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, no-wrap
msgid "Quantum computer: A quantum computer is comprised of the QPU plus the classical compute which hosts the runtime environment.\n"
msgstr "Komputer kuantum: Komputer kuantum terdiri daripada QPU ditambah pengiraan klasik yang mengehoskan persekitaran masa jalan.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, no-wrap
msgid "Runtime environment: The combination of hardware and software that makes it possible to run a program.\n"
msgstr "Persekitaran masa jalan: Gabungan perkakasan dan perisian yang memungkinkan untuk menjalankan program.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Layers in quantum circuits {#layers-in-quantum-circuits}"
msgstr "Lapisan dalam litar kuantum {#layers-in-quantum-circuits}"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "In both classical and quantum computing, processes could be executed sequentially or in parallel. Because qubits have a rich state space compared to classical bits, it sometimes makes sense for multiple single-qubit gates to be executed on a qubit in sequence (like an `R_x` gate followed by an `R_z` gate). Since entanglement between qubits is critical to quantum computing, it is also common for a quantum circuit to have a set of entangling gates acting across many qubits. These and other factors make it common to identify processes that can be executed in parallel on the scale of individual gate operations in a quantum circuit. In classical computing, bit-level parallelism is also possible but less commonly considered at the gate level; it is more common to refer to parallel and sequential processes at a larger scale.\n"
msgstr "Dalam pengiraan klasik dan kuantum, proses boleh dilaksanakan secara berurutan atau selari. Kerana qubit mempunyai ruang keadaan yang kaya berbanding bit klasik, kadangkala masuk akal untuk beberapa get satu-qubit dilaksanakan pada qubit secara berurutan (seperti get `R_x` diikuti dengan get `R_z`). Memandangkan keterjeratan antara qubit adalah kritikal dalam pengiraan kuantum, adalah perkara biasa untuk litar kuantum mempunyai set get pembelit yang bertindak merentasi banyak qubit. Faktor-faktor ini dan lainnya menjadikannya perkara biasa untuk mengenal pasti proses yang boleh dilaksanakan secara selari pada skala operasi get individu dalam litar kuantum. Dalam pengiraan klasik, keselarian peringkat bit juga mungkin tetapi kurang lazim dipertimbangkan pada peringkat get; adalah lebih biasa untuk merujuk kepada proses selari dan berurutan pada skala yang lebih besar.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, fuzzy, no-wrap
#| msgid "In quantum computing, one refers to a \"layer\" of gates which can all be executed simultaneously. In many applications it is useful to perform a set of rotations on all qubits and then entangling gates between pairs of qubits. In these contexts, one refers to a \"rotation layer\" (a layer of gates like `R_x`, `R-y`, and/or `R_z`) and an \"entangling layer\" (like one with `CNOT` gates). The number of layers in a circuit is the \"circuit depth\", an important measure since greater depth means more layers of compounding noise and errors.\n"
msgid "In quantum computing, one refers to a \"layer\" of gates which can all be executed simultaneously. In many applications it is useful to perform a set of rotations on all qubits and then entangling gates between pairs of qubits. In these contexts, one refers to a \"rotation layer\" (a layer of gates like `R_x`, `R_y`, and/or `R_z`) and an \"entangling layer\" (like one with `CNOT` gates). The number of layers in a circuit is the \"circuit depth\", an important measure since greater depth means more layers of compounding noise and errors.\n"
msgstr "Dalam pengiraan kuantum, seseorang merujuk kepada \"lapisan\" get yang semua boleh dilaksanakan serentak. Dalam banyak aplikasi, berguna untuk melakukan set putaran pada semua qubit dan kemudian get pembelit antara pasangan qubit. Dalam konteks ini, seseorang merujuk kepada \"lapisan putaran\" (lapisan get seperti `R_x`, `R-y`, dan/atau `R_z`) dan \"lapisan pembelit\" (seperti satu dengan get `CNOT`). Bilangan lapisan dalam litar ialah \"kedalaman litar\", ukuran penting kerana kedalaman yang lebih besar bermakna lebih banyak lapisan hingar dan ralat yang bergabung.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "It can be difficult to identify gate layers visually when the layers are not aligned using barriers. In Qiskit, a barrier serves as an instruction in quantum circuits that acts as a visual separator and a constraint during compilation. Both in drawing the circuit and executing it, no gates will be moved across the barrier. This can be important in contexts like dynamical decoupling, in which one intentionally implements gates that simplify to an identity to suppress certain error types. For more on dynamical decoupling see [this guide](/guides/error-mitigation-and-suppression-techniques). For the visual effect of barriers, compare these two images of the same circuit, the first without barriers and the second with barriers to force alignment of layers.\n"
msgstr "Boleh jadi sukar untuk mengenal pasti lapisan get secara visual apabila lapisan tidak disejajarkan menggunakan penghadang. Dalam Qiskit, penghadang berfungsi sebagai arahan dalam litar kuantum yang bertindak sebagai pemisah visual dan kekangan semasa penyusunan. Baik dalam melukis litar mahupun melaksanakannya, tiada get akan dipindahkan merentasi penghadang. Ini boleh menjadi penting dalam konteks seperti penyahgandingan dinamik, di mana seseorang dengan sengaja melaksanakan get yang memudahkan ke identiti untuk menekan jenis ralat tertentu. Untuk maklumat lanjut tentang penyahgandingan dinamik, lihat [panduan ini](/guides/error-mitigation-and-suppression-techniques). Untuk kesan visual penghadang, bandingkan dua imej litar yang sama ini, yang pertama tanpa penghadang dan yang kedua dengan penghadang untuk memaksa penjajaran lapisan.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![Four-qubit quantum circuit with no barriers to force alignment of layers; gates appear somewhat randomly aligned.](/learning/images/courses/integrating-quantum-and-high-performance-computing/compute-resources/unaligned.avif)\n"
msgstr "![Four-qubit quantum circuit with no barriers to force alignment of layers; gates appear somewhat randomly aligned.](/learning/images/courses/integrating-quantum-and-high-performance-computing/compute-resources/unaligned.avif)\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![Four-qubit quantum circuit with barriers in place to force the alignment of layers. Counting layers is much easier now.](/learning/images/courses/integrating-quantum-and-high-performance-computing/compute-resources/aligned.avif)\n"
msgstr "![Four-qubit quantum circuit with barriers in place to force the alignment of layers. Counting layers is much easier now.](/learning/images/courses/integrating-quantum-and-high-performance-computing/compute-resources/aligned.avif)\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"These are the same circuit and have the same number of layers. But in the second, the alignment makes it easy to see that the circuit has:\n"
"* Two rotation layers: one around the Y axis by $\\pi/5$, one around the Z axis by $\\pi/4$.\n"
"* Three entangling layers. Note that one can call each CNOT a \"layer\" on its own, since the CNOTs cannot be re-ordered to be parallel without changing the logical operation.\n"
"* Two more rotation layers: one around the Y axis by $\\pi/3$, one around the Z axis by $\\pi/2$.\n"
"* Two more entangling layers. Note that this time the first layer has been slightly more parallelized that in the first set of entangling layers.\n"
msgstr ""
"Ini adalah litar yang sama dan mempunyai bilangan lapisan yang sama. Tetapi dalam yang kedua, penjajaran memudahkan untuk melihat bahawa litar mempunyai:\n"
"* Dua lapisan putaran: satu di sekitar paksi Y sebanyak $\\pi/5$, satu di sekitar paksi Z sebanyak $\\pi/4$.\n"
"* Tiga lapisan pembelit. Perhatikan bahawa seseorang boleh menyebut setiap CNOT sebagai \"lapisan\" sendiri, kerana CNOT tidak boleh disusun semula untuk menjadi selari tanpa mengubah operasi logik.\n"
"* Dua lapisan putaran lagi: satu di sekitar paksi Y sebanyak $\\pi/3$, satu di sekitar paksi Z sebanyak $\\pi/2$.\n"
"* Dua lapisan pembelit lagi. Perhatikan bahawa kali ini lapisan pertama telah diselaraskan secara selari sedikit lebih berbanding set lapisan pembelit pertama.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The depth of each circuit is 9.\n"
msgstr "Kedalaman setiap litar ialah 9.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Units of measure {#units-of-measure}"
msgstr "Unit ukuran {#units-of-measure}"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "In quantum computing, the capabilities of a quantum system are often described using three fundamental dimensions: scale, quality, and speed. IBM Quantum characterizes these dimensions using three hardware metrics that can be applied across quantum-computing modalities: programmable qubits (scale), qubit operations (quality), and maximum circuits per second (speed). These metrics provide a high-level view of a system's computational capability and efficiency across different hardware implementations.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, no-wrap
msgid "**Scale** is measured by the number of programmable qubits. Programmable qubits are the qubits that users can directly control, manipulate, measure, and incorporate into quantum algorithms.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, no-wrap
msgid "**Quality** is measured by qubit operations (QuOps), a metric that describes how many of the most computationally demanding operations a system can reliably execute before accumulated errors overwhelm the computation. Higher QuOps capability generally enables deeper circuits and more sophisticated quantum algorithms.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, no-wrap
msgid "**Speed** is measured by maximum circuits per second, also known as circuit throughput. This metric captures how many useful quantum circuits a system can execute over time and provides an indication of overall computational efficiency.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Additional hardware metrics are often used to characterize specific aspects of processor behavior. For example, EPLG (errors per layered gate) and layer-fidelity measurements provide insight into gate quality and error accumulation, while CLOPS (Circuit Layer Operations Per Second) measures the execution speed of a standardized quantum benchmarking workload. These metrics remain useful for evaluating particular workloads and implementation details, but programmable qubits, qubit operations, and maximum circuits per second provide a broader framework for comparing quantum systems.\n"
msgstr ""

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Differences between quantum and classical metrics {#differences-between-quantum-and-classical-metrics}"
msgstr "Perbezaan antara metrik kuantum dan klasik {#differences-between-quantum-and-classical-metrics}"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Classical computing systems are often characterized using metrics such as FLOPS (floating-point operations per second), memory capacity, memory bandwidth, and processor clock rates. Some loose analogies can be made between these and the metrics for quantum computers introduced above, but the differences in metrics are important.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Maximum circuits per second can be viewed as a loose analog to throughput-oriented performance metrics in classical computing because it measures how much useful work a quantum system can perform over time. Unlike FLOPS, however, it measures execution of quantum circuits rather than arithmetic operations.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "CLOPS is sometimes compared to classical throughput benchmarks such as FLOPS, but it is best understood as a workload-specific benchmark rather than a universal measure of quantum-computing performance. EPLG and related fidelity measurements provide insight into how accurately a processor executes layers of quantum gates. These metrics are useful for understanding specific hardware characteristics, while the primary scale-quality-speed framework provides a more general basis for comparing quantum systems. Many descriptions of quantum computer performance can be found on the [Compute resources](https://quantum.cloud.ibm.com/computers) page on IBM Quantum Platform. Note that while the programmable qubit count is a fixed number for a given QPU, speed and quality metrics can vary slightly over time due to calibration updates, maintenance, operational conditions, and improvements to the surrounding hardware and software infrastructure.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Together, these complementary metrics help guide resource allocation, scheduling, and application design on quantum computing systems.\n"
msgstr ""

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, fuzzy, no-wrap
#| msgid "Together, these metrics guide how quantum systems are allocated and scheduled. In many cases, the entire quantum system is treated as a single unit. However, when a task exceeds the capacity of one unit—whether in terms of qubit count, circuit depth, or execution speed—techniques such as circuit cutting/knitting can be used. Circuit cutting is the process of breaks down large quantum tasks into smaller, manageable sub-tasks that can be distributed across multiple quantum chips, enabling scalable quantum computation despite hardware limitations. Circuit knitting refers to the process that comes after circuit cutting — the classical post-processing step that \"knits\" or combines the results from the smaller subcircuits back together.\n"
msgid "In many cases, the entire quantum system is treated as a single unit. However, when a task exceeds the capacity of one unit—whether in terms of programmable qubit count, circuit depth, or circuit throughput — techniques such as circuit cutting/knitting can be used. Circuit cutting is the process of breaking down large quantum tasks into smaller, manageable sub-tasks that can be distributed across multiple quantum chips, enabling scalable quantum computation despite hardware limitations. Circuit knitting refers to the process that comes after circuit cutting — the classical post-processing step that \"knits\" or combines the results from the smaller subcircuits back together.\n"
msgstr "Bersama-sama, metrik ini membimbing cara sistem kuantum diperuntukkan dan dijadualkan. Dalam banyak kes, keseluruhan sistem kuantum diperlakukan sebagai satu unit. Walau bagaimanapun, apabila tugas melebihi kapasiti satu unit — sama ada dari segi bilangan qubit, kedalaman litar, atau kelajuan pelaksanaan — teknik seperti pemotongan/penganyaman litar boleh digunakan. Pemotongan litar ialah proses memecah tugas kuantum besar kepada sub-tugas yang lebih kecil dan terurus yang boleh diedarkan merentasi berbilang cip kuantum, membolehkan pengiraan kuantum berskala walaupun dengan had perkakasan. Penganyaman litar merujuk kepada proses yang berlaku selepas pemotongan litar — langkah pasca-pemprosesan klasik yang \"menganyam\" atau menggabungkan semula keputusan daripada sub-litar yang lebih kecil.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Quantum computers do not have traditional memory, in the sense of persistent, addressable storage like RAM or GPU memory. Classical computing resources have discrete bits stored in memory, allowing data to be saved, fetched, and reused during computation. Quantum resources use qubits which do not store memory in the classical sense. Instead, qubits exist in quantum states that represent superpositions of 0 and 1 simultaneously, enabling exponential parallelism in state space. However, qubit states are fragile and cannot be cloned or read deterministically at intermediate steps without collapsing the quantum state, so persistent memory-like behavior during computation does not exist. Qubits must be maintained in a coherent state throughout execution, and the \"memory\" is essentially the quantum state itself. Classical memory can only be used alongside a quantum processor, not as internal quantum memory. This has significant implications: classical compute resources can reuse and store intermediate results freely; quantum resources cannot do this without measurements which disturb the computation.\n"
msgstr "Komputer kuantum tidak mempunyai ingatan tradisional, dalam erti storan yang boleh dialamatkan secara berterusan seperti RAM atau ingatan GPU. Sumber pengiraan klasik mempunyai bit diskret yang disimpan dalam ingatan, membolehkan data disimpan, diambil semula, dan digunakan semula semasa pengiraan. Sumber kuantum menggunakan qubit yang tidak menyimpan ingatan dalam erti klasik. Sebaliknya, qubit wujud dalam keadaan kuantum yang mewakili superposisi 0 dan 1 secara serentak, membolehkan kesejalanan eksponen dalam ruang keadaan. Walau bagaimanapun, keadaan qubit adalah rapuh dan tidak boleh diklon atau dibaca secara deterministik pada langkah pertengahan tanpa meruntuhkan keadaan kuantum, jadi tingkah laku seperti ingatan yang berterusan semasa pengiraan tidak wujud. qubit mesti dikekalkan dalam keadaan koheren sepanjang pelaksanaan, dan \"ingatan\" pada dasarnya ialah keadaan kuantum itu sendiri. Ingatan klasik hanya boleh digunakan bersama pemproses kuantum, bukan sebagai ingatan kuantum dalaman. Ini mempunyai implikasi yang signifikan: sumber pengiraan klasik boleh menggunakan semula dan menyimpan keputusan pertengahan dengan bebas; sumber kuantum tidak boleh melakukan ini tanpa pengukuran yang mengganggu pengiraan.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Connectivity to classical infrastructure {#connectivity-to-classical-infrastructure}"
msgstr "Sambungan ke infrastruktur klasik {#connectivity-to-classical-infrastructure}"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, fuzzy, no-wrap
#| msgid "QPUs can be connected to classical infrastructure through networks and various application programming interfaces (APIs) that allow software developers to interact with QPUs programmatically. These APIs are usually hidden behind software development kits (SDKs) and libraries (like Qiskit) and exposed to computational scientists in the form of programming abstractions (like Qiskit Primitives, which we will be talking about in Chapter 3: programming models).\n"
msgid "QPUs can be connected to classical infrastructure through networks and various application programming interfaces (APIs) that allow software developers to interact with QPUs programmatically. These APIs are usually hidden behind software development kits (SDKs) and libraries (like Qiskit) and exposed to computational scientists in the form of programming abstractions (like primitives, which we will be talking about in Chapter 3: programming models).\n"
msgstr "QPU boleh disambungkan ke infrastruktur klasik melalui rangkaian dan pelbagai antara muka pengaturcaraan aplikasi (API) yang membolehkan pembangun perisian berinteraksi dengan QPU secara pengaturcaraan. API ini biasanya tersembunyi di sebalik kit pembangunan perisian (SDK) dan pustaka (seperti Qiskit) dan didedahkan kepada saintis pengiraan dalam bentuk abstraksi pengaturcaraan (seperti Primitif Qiskit, yang akan kita bincangkan dalam Bab 3: model pengaturcaraan).\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "It is worth drawing a distinction between tight and loose integration of quantum and classical resources. Currently QPUs are not on the same node as classical compute resources. In fact, QPUs are not currently connected via PCIe, but via the network. This could change in the future, but there are engineering challenges related to environmental conditions optimal for QPUs and classical compute resources.\n"
msgstr "Adalah wajar untuk membuat perbezaan antara integrasi ketat dan longgar sumber kuantum dan klasik. Pada masa ini QPU tidak berada pada nod yang sama dengan sumber pengiraan klasik. Malah, QPU pada masa ini tidak disambungkan melalui PCIe, tetapi melalui rangkaian. Ini mungkin berubah pada masa hadapan, tetapi terdapat cabaran kejuruteraan berkaitan keadaan persekitaran yang optimum untuk QPU dan sumber pengiraan klasik.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Scaling quantum resources {#scaling-quantum-resources}"
msgstr "Menskalakan sumber kuantum {#scaling-quantum-resources}"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Scaling of quantum resources can also be categorized into vertical and horizontal.\n"
msgstr "Penskalaan sumber kuantum juga boleh dikategorikan kepada menegak dan mendatar.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, no-wrap
msgid "Vertical scaling would be increasing the number of qubits per chip or improving the fidelity of devices.\n"
msgstr "Penskalaan menegak adalah meningkatkan bilangan qubit setiap cip atau meningkatkan kesetiaan peranti.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, no-wrap
msgid "Horizontal scaling would be connecting chips with couplers or with classical interconnect.\n"
msgstr "Penskalaan mendatar adalah menghubungkan cip dengan gandingan atau dengan interkoneksi klasik.\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![An image showing vertical scaling of quantum resources as more qubits on a chip, and horizontal scaling of quantum resources as connecting many chips together with couplers.](/learning/images/courses/integrating-quantum-and-high-performance-computing/compute-resources/quantum-scaling.avif)\n"
msgstr "![An image showing vertical scaling of quantum resources as more qubits on a chip, and horizontal scaling of quantum resources as connecting many chips together with couplers.](/learning/images/courses/integrating-quantum-and-high-performance-computing/compute-resources/quantum-scaling.avif)\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Check your understanding {#check-your-understanding-1}"
msgstr "Uji kefahaman anda {#check-your-understanding-1}"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "What are the quantum analogs of classical (a) bits of information, and (b) processor speed?\n"
msgstr "Apakah analog kuantum bagi (a) bit maklumat klasik, dan (b) kelajuan pemproses?\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"(a) Quantum bits or qubits - units of information which unlike their classical counterparts (which can only adopt the state 0 or 1), can be in a superposition of 0 and 1 simultaneously.\n"
"(b) Circuit layer operations per second or CLOPS - number of sequential operations the QPU can perform each second, including some interfacing with classical computing resources, as in loading parameters from the circuit.\n"
msgstr "(a) Bit kuantum atau qubit — unit maklumat yang berbeza daripada rakan sejawat klasik mereka (yang hanya boleh berada dalam keadaan 0 atau 1), boleh berada dalam superposisi 0 dan 1 secara serentak.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Title ##
#, no-wrap
msgid "Resource management {#resource-management}"
msgstr "Pengurusan sumber {#resource-management}"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Both HPC and quantum resources are both precious and complex; they must be managed carefully. In this section, we will explain how to manage resources for user programs. Resource management in compute infrastructure refers to the process of (1) planning, (2) allocating, and (3) controlling/managing the use of computing resources such as CPUs, memory, storage, and network bandwidth to ensure efficient and effective resource utilization.\n"
msgstr "Sumber HPC dan kuantum adalah berharga dan kompleks; ia mesti diuruskan dengan teliti. Dalam bahagian ini, kita akan menerangkan cara mengurus sumber untuk program pengguna. Pengurusan sumber dalam infrastruktur pengiraan merujuk kepada proses (1) perancangan, (2) peruntukan, dan (3) kawalan/pengurusan penggunaan sumber pengiraan seperti CPU, ingatan, storan, dan lebar jalur rangkaian untuk memastikan penggunaan sumber yang cekap dan berkesan.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Planning - resource estimation {#planning-resource-estimation}"
msgstr "Perancangan - anggaran sumber {#planning-resource-estimation}"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Any program consumes resources, and estimating the resources required is crucial for efficient resource management. This includes estimating the amount of CPU, memory, and other resources needed to execute a program. The same can be said for quantum resources. However, quantum resources exist on a completely different scale. IBM Quantum&reg; Heron r3 quantum processors have 156 qubits, compared to the many billions of classical bits on a common laptop. Time and cost are also considerations. Currently, IBM Quantum has a free plan, the [Open Plan](https://www.ibm.com/quantum/products#access-plans), that allows users to explore quantum computing using 10 minutes of QPU time per month. Some research organizations require so much QPU time that they have a dedicated IBM quantum computer on premises.\n"
msgstr "Mana-mana program menggunakan sumber, dan menganggarkan sumber yang diperlukan adalah penting untuk pengurusan sumber yang cekap. Ini termasuk menganggarkan jumlah CPU, ingatan, dan sumber lain yang diperlukan untuk melaksanakan program. Perkara yang sama boleh dikatakan untuk sumber kuantum. Walau bagaimanapun, sumber kuantum wujud pada skala yang sama sekali berbeza. Pemproses kuantum IBM Quantum&reg; Heron r3 mempunyai 156 qubit, berbanding banyak bilion bit klasik pada laptop biasa. Masa dan kos juga merupakan pertimbangan. Pada masa ini, IBM Quantum mempunyai pelan percuma, [Pelan Terbuka](https://www.ibm.com/quantum/products#access-plans), yang membolehkan pengguna meneroka pengiraan kuantum menggunakan 10 minit masa QPU setiap bulan. Beberapa organisasi penyelidikan memerlukan begitu banyak masa QPU sehingga mereka mempunyai komputer kuantum IBM yang didedikasikan di premis.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "One step in resource estimation that is unique to quantum computing is the circuit depth. As mentioned earlier, each quantum gate and each delay time between operations comes with noise and a certain probability of an error. The deeper the quantum circuit, the greater the noise. There are two subtleties to this: two-qubit gates have much higher error rates than single-qubit gates, so one can often ignore the single-qubit depth. Further, not all qubits on a quantum chip are directly connected. Sometimes information has to be swapped from qubit to qubit in order to perform the required entanglements and this swapping process itself requires two-qubit gates. That swapping is handled in a process called \"transpilation\", a complex process that serves other purposes as well; this is discussed in more detail in the next lesson. The relevant limiting quantity is thus the transpiled two-qubit depth. The exact maximum depth at which high-fidelity results can be obtained depends on the circuit. But leveraging modern error mitigation techniques, one can obtain high-fidelity results with transpiled two-qubit depths of 80 or more.\n"
msgstr "Satu langkah dalam anggaran sumber yang unik untuk pengiraan kuantum ialah kedalaman litar. Seperti yang dinyatakan sebelum ini, setiap get kuantum dan setiap masa kelewatan antara operasi datang dengan hingar dan kebarangkalian ralat tertentu. Semakin dalam litar kuantum, semakin besar hingarnya. Terdapat dua kerumitan di sini: get dua-qubit mempunyai kadar ralat yang jauh lebih tinggi daripada get satu-qubit, jadi seseorang sering boleh mengabaikan kedalaman satu-qubit. Lebih lanjut, tidak semua qubit pada cip kuantum disambungkan secara langsung. Kadangkala maklumat perlu ditukar dari qubit ke qubit untuk melakukan keterjeratan yang diperlukan dan proses penukaran itu sendiri memerlukan get dua-qubit. Penukaran itu dikendalikan dalam proses yang dipanggil \"transpilasi\", proses kompleks yang juga mempunyai tujuan lain; ini dibincangkan dengan lebih terperinci dalam pelajaran seterusnya. Kuantiti pengehad yang berkaitan ialah kedalaman dua-qubit yang telah ditranspilasi. Kedalaman maksimum yang tepat di mana keputusan berkesetiaan tinggi boleh diperoleh bergantung pada litar. Tetapi dengan memanfaatkan teknik mitigasi ralat moden, seseorang boleh mendapatkan keputusan berkesetiaan tinggi dengan kedalaman dua-qubit yang telah ditranspilasi sebanyak 80 atau lebih.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Allocating - scheduling {#allocating-scheduling}"
msgstr "Peruntukan - penjadualan {#allocating-scheduling}"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Scheduling is the process of allocating resources to programs and managing their execution. This involves:\n"
"* Job submission: The process by which a user sends a request (job) to the HPC system, specifying what computational work and resources are needed for execution.\n"
"* Resource allocation: The assignment of available HPC system resources (such as nodes, CPUs, memory) to a submitted job based on its requirements.\n"
"* Job execution: The actual running of the computational tasks defined by the job on the allocated HPC resources.\n"
msgstr ""
"Penjadualan ialah proses memperuntukkan sumber kepada program dan mengurus pelaksanaan mereka. Ini melibatkan:\n"
"* Penghantaran kerja: Proses di mana pengguna menghantar permintaan (kerja) kepada sistem HPC, menentukan kerja pengiraan dan sumber yang diperlukan untuk pelaksanaan.\n"
"* Peruntukan sumber: Penugasan sumber sistem HPC yang tersedia (seperti nod, CPU, ingatan) kepada kerja yang dihantar berdasarkan keperluannya.\n"
"* Pelaksanaan kerja: Pelaksanaan sebenar tugas pengiraan yang ditakrifkan oleh kerja pada sumber HPC yang diperuntukkan.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, fuzzy, no-wrap
#| msgid ""
#| "There are analogs of all these processes for quantum computers.\n"
#| "* Jobs are submitted by the user, leveraging Qiskit Runtime, and typically using a Qiskit Runtime primitive, like `Sampler`, `Estimator`, or others.\n"
#| "* The user selects from a list of backends to which they have access. The complete list of available backends can be seen on the [Compute resources](https://quantum.cloud.ibm.com/computers) page on IBM Quantum Platform. It is common to simply use the least busy quantum computer. But there are cases where it might be important to use a specific one because of device layout considerations, replication of previous calculations, and so on.\n"
#| "* The execution of quantum jobs is similar to the HPC case. Although some differences have already been outlined, a few are worth repeating here. QPUs are currently not generally located on the same node as classical compute resources but are connected through a network. This might have scheduling implications. Further, quantum computers might have substantial queue times, and these queue times vary, making precising control of timing difficult. This situation might be different for dedicated systems; that depends on the quantum computer's internal administration.\n"
msgid ""
"There are analogs of all these processes for quantum computers.\n"
"* Jobs are submitted by the user, leveraging IBM Quantum Compute Service, and typically using an IBM Quantum primitive, like `Sampler`, `Estimator`, or others.\n"
"* The user selects from a list of backends to which they have access. The complete list of available backends can be seen on the [Compute resources](https://quantum.cloud.ibm.com/computers) page on IBM Quantum Platform. It is common to simply use the least busy quantum computer. But there are cases where it might be important to use a specific one because of device layout considerations, replication of previous calculations, and so on.\n"
"* The execution of quantum jobs is similar to the HPC case. Although some differences have already been outlined, a few are worth repeating here. QPUs are currently not generally located on the same node as classical compute resources but are connected through a network. This might have scheduling implications. Further, quantum computers might have substantial queue times, and these queue times vary, making precising control of timing difficult. This situation might be different for dedicated systems; that depends on the quantum computer's internal administration.\n"
msgstr ""
"Terdapat analog semua proses ini untuk komputer kuantum.\n"
"* Kerja dihantar oleh pengguna, memanfaatkan Qiskit Runtime, dan biasanya menggunakan primitif Qiskit Runtime, seperti `Sampler`, `Estimator`, atau lainnya.\n"
"* Pengguna memilih daripada senarai backend yang boleh mereka akses. Senarai lengkap backend yang tersedia boleh dilihat pada halaman [Sumber pengiraan](https://quantum.cloud.ibm.com/computers) di IBM Quantum Platform. Adalah lazim untuk hanya menggunakan komputer kuantum yang paling tidak sibuk. Tetapi terdapat kes di mana mungkin penting untuk menggunakan yang tertentu kerana pertimbangan susun atur peranti, replikasi pengiraan sebelumnya, dan sebagainya.\n"
"* Pelaksanaan kerja kuantum adalah serupa dengan kes HPC. Walaupun beberapa perbezaan telah digariskan, beberapa wajar diulang di sini. QPU pada masa ini tidak terletak secara umum pada nod yang sama dengan sumber pengiraan klasik tetapi disambungkan melalui rangkaian. Ini mungkin mempunyai implikasi penjadualan. Lebih lanjut, komputer kuantum mungkin mempunyai masa giliran yang besar, dan masa giliran ini berbeza-beza, menjadikan kawalan tepat masa sukar. Keadaan ini mungkin berbeza untuk sistem yang didedikasikan; itu bergantung pada pentadbiran dalaman komputer kuantum.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Controlling/Managing - workload management {#controllingmanaging-workload-management}"
msgstr "Kawalan/Pengurusan - pengurusan beban kerja {#controllingmanaging-workload-management}"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Workload management, also known as orchestration, is the process of managing multiple programs and their resource requirements. This involves:\n"
"* Resource provisioning: The process of preparing and making HPC resources available and ready for use by jobs, including hardware and software setup. As we will see later, QPUs are computing resources that can be provisioned similarly to classical HPC resources, with the caveats from the previous section.\n"
"* Job scheduling: The activity of the scheduler software in deciding which jobs run, when, and on which resources, managing priorities and queues to efficiently utilize the HPC system. Although this broad statement applies to quantum resources, there could be less control over timing than with other resources.\n"
msgstr ""
"Pengurusan beban kerja, juga dikenali sebagai orkestrasi, ialah proses mengurus berbilang program dan keperluan sumber mereka. Ini melibatkan:\n"
"* Penyediaan sumber: Proses menyediakan dan membuat sumber HPC tersedia dan sedia untuk digunakan oleh kerja, termasuk persediaan perkakasan dan perisian. Seperti yang akan kita lihat kemudian, QPU adalah sumber pengiraan yang boleh disediakan dengan cara yang serupa dengan sumber HPC klasik, dengan amaran dari bahagian sebelumnya.\n"
"* Penjadualan kerja: Aktiviti perisian penjadual dalam memutuskan kerja mana yang dijalankan, bila, dan pada sumber mana, mengurus keutamaan dan giliran untuk menggunakan sistem HPC dengan cekap. Walaupun pernyataan luas ini terpakai pada sumber kuantum, mungkin terdapat kawalan yang kurang ke atas masa berbanding dengan sumber lain.\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![An image showing workloads (shown as boxes) being organized and arranged to fit optimally into a two dimensional grid with one axis representing time and the other representing resources.](/learning/images/courses/integrating-quantum-and-high-performance-computing/compute-resources/resource-manage.avif)\n"
"**Example:**\n"
msgstr ""
"![An image showing workloads (shown as boxes) being organized and arranged to fit optimally into a two dimensional grid with one axis representing time and the other representing resources.](/learning/images/courses/integrating-quantum-and-high-performance-computing/compute-resources/resource-manage.avif)\n"
"**Example:**\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Consider a well-known task as a context for understanding resource management: finding the prime factors of large numbers. Let us further assume that the algorithm being used relies on brute force checking of every potential divisor. While this is often not the most efficient method, it is easy to understand how the workload might be managed.\n"
msgstr "Pertimbangkan tugas yang terkenal sebagai konteks untuk memahami pengurusan sumber: mencari faktor perdana bagi nombor besar. Mari kita andaikan lebih lanjut bahawa algoritma yang digunakan bergantung pada pemeriksaan paksa setiap pembahagi yang berpotensi. Walaupun ini sering bukan kaedah yang paling cekap, mudah untuk memahami bagaimana beban kerja mungkin diuruskan.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Planning - resource estimation\n"
msgstr "Perancangan - anggaran sumber\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, no-wrap
msgid "Estimate how much CPU time and memory the prime factorization might require.\n"
msgstr "Anggaran berapa banyak masa CPU dan ingatan yang diperlukan oleh pemfaktoran perdana.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, no-wrap
msgid "Plan the parallelization of your task - how many CPUs/cores will you use?\n"
msgstr "Rancang peselarian tugas anda — berapa banyak CPU/teras yang akan anda gunakan?\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Allocating - scheduling\n"
msgstr "Peruntukan - penjadualan\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, no-wrap
msgid "Upon job submission, the scheduler assigns CPU cores and memory to the prime factorization task. For example, it might allocate all potential divisors ending in the digits `1, 3, 7, 9` to one of four cores, respectively.\n"
msgstr "Apabila kerja dihantar, penjadual memperuntukkan teras CPU dan ingatan kepada tugas pemfaktoran perdana. Sebagai contoh, ia mungkin memperuntukkan semua pembahagi berpotensi yang berakhir dengan digit `1, 3, 7, 9` kepada satu daripada empat teras, masing-masing.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, no-wrap
msgid "Job execution: The prime factorization algorithm runs, performing divisions or other factorization steps on the allocated resources until the task completes.\n"
msgstr "Pelaksanaan kerja: Algoritma pemfaktoran perdana berjalan, melakukan pembahagian atau langkah pemfaktoran lain pada sumber yang diperuntukkan sehingga tugas selesai.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Controlling/Managing - workload management\n"
msgstr "Kawalan/Pengurusan - pengurusan beban kerja\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, no-wrap
msgid "The system orchestrates the order and timing of prime factorization jobs to optimize throughput.\n"
msgstr "Sistem mengatur tertib dan masa kerja pemfaktoran perdana untuk mengoptimumkan daya pemprosesan.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, no-wrap
msgid "The easiest case to imagine is that one of the cores finds the target prime factor. This should stop the calculation on other cores so they can be used for the next task.\n"
msgstr "Kes yang paling mudah dibayangkan ialah salah satu teras menemui faktor perdana sasaran. Ini seharusnya menghentikan pengiraan pada teras lain supaya ia boleh digunakan untuk tugas seterusnya.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "High-performance computing environments use special software to carry out these steps and manage resources. In the next section, we will learn about a widely-adopted resource management software systems: Slurm.\n"
msgstr "Persekitaran pengiraan berprestasi tinggi menggunakan perisian khas untuk menjalankan langkah-langkah ini dan mengurus sumber. Dalam bahagian seterusnya, kita akan belajar tentang sistem perisian pengurusan sumber yang diterima pakai secara meluas: Slurm.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "**Example with quantum resources:**\n"
msgstr "**Contoh dengan sumber kuantum:**\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"A workflow that will be the subject of other lessons in this course is determining chemical ground states and energies using sample-based quantum diagonalization (SQD). This is covered in more detail in Lesson 4, and you can also visit [this course on SQD](/learning/courses/quantum-diagonalization-algorithms) and related methods on [IBM Quantum Learning](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/learning/). All we need to know for this discussion is that the workflow involves the following:\n"
"* Prepare a quantum circuit\n"
"* Measure the quantum circuit\n"
"* Use the measurement results to project the problem into a useful subspace\n"
"* Diagonalize a smaller, projected matrix using classical computing resources\n"
"* Iteration, either to ensure self-consistency through considerations like charge conservation, and possible iterations of the quantum circuit if it has variational parameters.\n"
msgstr ""
"Aliran kerja yang akan menjadi subjek pelajaran lain dalam kursus ini ialah menentukan keadaan asas kimia dan tenaga menggunakan pendiagonalan kuantum berasaskan sampel (SQD). Ini diliputi dengan lebih terperinci dalam Pelajaran 4, dan anda juga boleh melawati [kursus ini mengenai SQD](/learning/courses/quantum-diagonalization-algorithms) dan kaedah berkaitan di [IBM Quantum Learning](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/learning/). Semua yang perlu kita ketahui untuk perbincangan ini ialah aliran kerja melibatkan perkara berikut:\n"
"* Sediakan litar kuantum\n"
"* Ukur litar kuantum\n"
"* Gunakan keputusan pengukuran untuk mengunjurkan masalah ke dalam subruang yang berguna\n"
"* Pepenjurukan matriks yang lebih kecil yang diunjurkan menggunakan sumber pengiraan klasik\n"
"* Lelaran, sama ada untuk memastikan konsistensi diri melalui pertimbangan seperti pemuliharaan cas, dan kemungkinan lelaran litar kuantum jika ia mempunyai parameter variasi.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, no-wrap
msgid "Map the electronic orbitals to qubits to establish the number of qubits you need.\n"
msgstr "Petakan orbit elektronik ke qubit untuk menetapkan bilangan qubit yang anda perlukan.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, no-wrap
msgid "Combine the mapped Hamiltonian of the system and the (possibly variational) state into a quantum circuit and check the transpiled, two-qubit depth. Ensure it is reasonable.\n"
msgstr "Gabungkan Hamiltonian sistem yang dipetakan dan keadaan (mungkin variasi) ke dalam litar kuantum dan periksa kedalaman dua-qubit yang telah ditranspilasi. Pastikan ia munasabah.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, no-wrap
msgid "Estimate the size of the subspace into which you will project; from this, estimate much CPU time and memory the diagonalization might require.\n"
msgstr "Anggaran saiz subruang ke mana anda akan mengunjurkan; daripada ini, anggaran berapa banyak masa CPU dan ingatan yang diperlukan oleh pepenjurusan.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, no-wrap
msgid "The user selects the QPU; the process of transpilation automatically maps qubits in your abstract quantum circuit to physical qubits on the QPU. This is important since the abstract circuit might assume direct connectivity that does not exist on the chip, among other reasons.\n"
msgstr "Pengguna memilih QPU; proses transpilasi secara automatik memetakan qubit dalam litar kuantum abstrak anda ke qubit fizikal pada QPU. Ini penting kerana litar abstrak mungkin mengandaikan sambungan langsung yang tidak wujud pada cip, antara sebab lain.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, fuzzy, no-wrap
#| msgid "Upon job submission via Qiskit Runtime, the job enters the queue for the selected QPU. The user has no control over the queue time, though this might be different for dedicated systems.\n"
msgid "Upon job submission via Quantum Compute, the job enters the queue for the selected QPU. The user has no control over the queue time, though this might be different for dedicated systems.\n"
msgstr "Apabila kerja dihantar melalui Qiskit Runtime, kerja memasuki giliran untuk QPU yang dipilih. Pengguna tidak mempunyai kawalan ke atas masa giliran, walaupun ini mungkin berbeza untuk sistem yang didedikasikan.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, no-wrap
msgid "Classical computing resources await the quantum results.\n"
msgstr "Sumber pengiraan klasik menunggu keputusan kuantum.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, no-wrap
msgid "A diagonalization job is submitted to HPC resources; upon job submission, the scheduler assigns CPU cores and memory to the diagonalization task.\n"
msgstr "Kerja pepenjurusan dihantar ke sumber HPC; apabila kerja dihantar, penjadual memperuntukkan teras CPU dan ingatan kepada tugas pepenjurusan.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, no-wrap
msgid "Job execution: The diagonalization algorithm runs, diagonalizing the smaller projected matrix until the task completes.\n"
msgstr "Pelaksanaan kerja: Algoritma pepenjurusan berjalan, mendiagonalkan matriks yang lebih kecil yang diunjurkan sehingga tugas selesai.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, no-wrap
msgid "The system orchestrates the order and timing of quantum and classical steps throughout. For example, once the projected matrix has been diagonalized and a ground state energy obtained, depending on convergence criteria, the workflow could loop back to a new quantum circuit (with a new variational parameter).\n"
msgstr "Sistem mengatur tertib dan masa langkah kuantum dan klasik sepanjang proses. Sebagai contoh, setelah matriks yang diunjurkan telah didiagonalkan dan tenaga keadaan asas diperoleh, bergantung pada kriteria konvergen, aliran kerja mungkin berulang kembali ke litar kuantum baharu (dengan parameter variasi baharu).\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, no-wrap
msgid "When convergence criteria are met by the ground state energy, the calculation on all cores stops.\n"
msgstr "Apabila kriteria konvergen dipenuhi oleh tenaga keadaan asas, pengiraan pada semua teras berhenti.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "High-performance computing environments use special software to carry out these steps and manage resources. In the next section, we will learn about a widely-adopted resource management software systems: Slurm. It is important to note that Slurm does not have tools for all steps described above. Slurm does not provide support for planning jobs, nor detailed workload management such as communication between workload components. This is well-suited to the current state of quantum computing in HPC, since QPUs are typically accessed over the network.\n"
msgstr "Persekitaran pengiraan berprestasi tinggi menggunakan perisian khas untuk menjalankan langkah-langkah ini dan mengurus sumber. Dalam bahagian seterusnya, kita akan belajar tentang sistem perisian pengurusan sumber yang diterima pakai secara meluas: Slurm. Penting untuk diperhatikan bahawa Slurm tidak mempunyai alat untuk semua langkah yang diterangkan di atas. Slurm tidak menyediakan sokongan untuk merancang kerja, mahupun pengurusan beban kerja terperinci seperti komunikasi antara komponen beban kerja. Ini sangat sesuai dengan keadaan semasa pengiraan kuantum dalam HPC, kerana QPU biasanya diakses melalui rangkaian.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Check your understanding {#check-your-understanding-2}"
msgstr "Uji kefahaman anda {#check-your-understanding-2}"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Suppose you are trying to search an unsorted database to find an element which we will call the 'target'. For each of the following actions, state to which stage of resource management it corresponds:\n"
msgstr ""
"Andaikan anda cuba mencari dalam pangkalan data yang tidak tersusun untuk mencari elemen yang kita sebut 'sasaran'. Untuk setiap tindakan berikut, nyatakan peringkat pengurusan sumber mana ia berpadanan:\n"
"(a) Menganggarkan saiz pangkalan data dan masa yang diperlukan untuk menyemak setiap elemen\n"
"(b) Memastikan bahawa mencari sasaran pada satu GPU menghentikan proses pada GPU lain untuk membebaskannya bagi masalah seterusnya.\n"
"(c) Memecah ruang carian kepada kawasan untuk setiap (katakan 10) GPU anda cari\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "(a) Estimating the size of the database and time required to check each element\n"
msgstr ""
"(a) Perancangan\n"
"(b) Kawalan/pengurusan\n"
"(c) Peruntukan/penjadualan,\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "(b) Ensuring that finding the target on one GPU stops the process on other GPUs to free them up for the next problem\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "(c) Splitting the search space into regions for each of your (say 10) GPUs to search\n"
msgstr ""

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "(a) Planning\n"
msgstr "(a) Planning\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "(b) Controlling/managing\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "(c) Allocating/scheduling\n"
msgstr ""

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Title ##
#, no-wrap
msgid "Software: Slurm {#software-slurm}"
msgstr "Perisian: Slurm {#software-slurm}"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "In this section, we will apply the concepts learned in this chapter to practice using the popular resource management system, Slurm.\n"
msgstr "Dalam bahagian ini, kita akan mengaplikasikan konsep yang dipelajari dalam bab ini untuk berlatih menggunakan sistem pengurusan sumber yang popular, Slurm.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Introduction to Slurm {#introduction-to-slurm}"
msgstr "Pengenalan kepada Slurm {#introduction-to-slurm}"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Slurm is an open-source resource management system widely used in high-performance computing environments. It provides a comprehensive set of tools for managing resources, scheduling jobs, and monitoring system performance.\n"
msgstr "Slurm ialah sistem pengurusan sumber sumber terbuka yang digunakan secara meluas dalam persekitaran pengiraan berprestasi tinggi. Ia menyediakan set alat yang komprehensif untuk mengurus sumber, menjadualkan kerja, dan memantau prestasi sistem.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"We will cover the basics of using Slurm, including:\n"
"* Job submission\n"
"* Resource allocation\n"
"* Job monitoring\n"
msgstr ""
"Kita akan merangkumi asas penggunaan Slurm, termasuk:\n"
"* Penghantaran kerja\n"
"* Peruntukan sumber\n"
"* Pemantauan kerja\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Since it is really hard to provide HPC resources to each student of this course, we will cheat a bit and provide you a repository with Docker images that are mimicking an actual HPC cluster with Slurm, but on a small scale. It will help us to practice learned concepts in safe, reproducible environments.\n"
msgstr "Memandangkan sukar untuk menyediakan sumber HPC kepada setiap pelajar kursus ini, kita akan sedikit menipu dengan menyediakan anda repositori dengan imej Docker yang meniru kluster HPC sebenar dengan Slurm, tetapi pada skala kecil. Ini akan membantu kita untuk mempraktikkan konsep yang dipelajari dalam persekitaran yang selamat dan boleh dihasilkan semula.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, fuzzy, no-wrap
#| msgid "Note that currently all quantum and classical resources are allocated for the duration of the whole experiment. Currently there is no interleaved allocation of mixed resources. A final caveat is that even once the job launches, the quantum system will not be directly controlled as a frequent HPC user might expect. The job is launched on an arbitrary x86-node, which executes the Qiskit Runtime service and that runtime service connects to another scheduler over which the user has no direct control. This workflow and related issues might be known to HPC users who had early experience pursuing exclusive access to GPU nodes (the original use of `gres`).\n"
msgid "Note that currently all quantum and classical resources are allocated for the duration of the whole experiment. Currently there is no interleaved allocation of mixed resources. A final caveat is that even once the job launches, the quantum system will not be directly controlled as a frequent HPC user might expect. The job is launched on an arbitrary x86-node, which executes IBM Quantum Compute Service and that runtime service connects to another scheduler over which the user has no direct control. This workflow and related issues might be known to HPC users who had early experience pursuing exclusive access to GPU nodes (the original use of `gres`).\n"
msgstr "Perhatikan bahawa pada masa ini semua sumber kuantum dan klasik diperuntukkan untuk tempoh keseluruhan eksperimen. Pada masa ini tiada peruntukan berselang-seli sumber campuran. Peringatan terakhir ialah walaupun setelah kerja dilancarkan, sistem kuantum tidak akan dikawal secara langsung seperti yang dijangkakan oleh pengguna HPC yang kerap. Kerja dilancarkan pada nod x86 sewenang-wenangnya, yang melaksanakan perkhidmatan Qiskit Runtime dan perkhidmatan masa jalan itu menyambung ke penjadual lain yang tidak berada dalam kawalan langsung pengguna. Aliran kerja dan isu berkaitan ini mungkin diketahui oleh pengguna HPC yang mempunyai pengalaman awal mengejar akses eksklusif ke nod GPU (penggunaan asal `gres`).\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Installation instructions and overview of setup {#installation-instructions-and-overview-of-setup}"
msgstr "Arahan pemasangan dan gambaran keseluruhan persediaan {#installation-instructions-and-overview-of-setup}"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "In order to practice combining quantum and HPC resources, you will either need access to a real HPC environment or you will need to simulate an HPC environment on your local machine. An installation guide for local setup using Docker can be found in [this repository](https://github.com/christopherporter1/hpc-course-demos). The guide links out to support for setting up an IBM Cloud&reg; account and installing the SPANK plugin for QRMI. Also in that repository are several Python files to test your environment.\n"
msgstr "Untuk berlatih menggabungkan sumber kuantum dan HPC, anda sama ada memerlukan akses ke persekitaran HPC sebenar atau perlu mensimulasikan persekitaran HPC pada mesin tempatan anda. Panduan pemasangan untuk persediaan tempatan menggunakan Docker boleh didapati di [repositori ini](https://github.com/christopherporter1/hpc-course-demos). Panduan tersebut menghubungkan ke sokongan untuk menyediakan akaun IBM Cloud&reg; dan memasang plugin SPANK untuk QRMI. Juga dalam repositori tersebut terdapat beberapa fail Python untuk menguji persekitaran anda.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Once you have completed the installation, let's use the command below to check the compute resource of Slurm at your terminal. If the installation was successful, you should be able to confirm a total of three virtual nodes.\n"
msgstr "Setelah anda selesai pemasangan, mari gunakan arahan di bawah untuk memeriksa sumber pengiraan Slurm di terminal anda. Jika pemasangan berjaya, anda seharusnya dapat mengesahkan jumlah tiga nod maya.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "We have two partitions or node groups: normal and quantum. The normal partition is composed of nodes that have access to classical resources only. The quantum partition has access to quantum resources. You can see the details of each node by executing `scontrol show nodes`.\n"
msgstr "Kita mempunyai dua partition atau kumpulan nod: normal dan quantum. Partition normal terdiri daripada nod yang hanya mempunyai akses ke sumber klasik. Partition quantum mempunyai akses ke sumber kuantum. Anda boleh melihat butiran setiap nod dengan melaksanakan `scontrol show nodes`.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Run a simple hello world example in Slurm {#run-a-simple-hello-world-example-in-slurm}"
msgstr "Jalankan contoh hello world klasik yang mudah dalam Slurm {#run-a-simple-hello-world-example-in-slurm}"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Let's first run a simple classical `hello world` example with Slurm. We will use Python for examples. Let's create hello_world.py, which is self-explanatory.\n"
msgstr "Mari jalankan dahulu contoh `hello world` klasik yang mudah dengan Slurm. Kita akan menggunakan Python untuk contoh. Mari buat hello_world.py, yang sudah jelas.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Now we need to tell the resource manager what resources we need to execute this program. Slurm provides a way to specify all metadata for the job via a submission script, which is just a shell script with Slurm-specific annotations. These annotations allow you to specify resource requirements, schedule parameters, and more. Let's create a `hello_world.sh` shell script for this.\n"
msgstr "Sekarang kita perlu memberitahu pengurus sumber sumber apa yang diperlukan untuk melaksanakan program ini. Slurm menyediakan cara untuk menentukan semua metadata bagi kerja melalui skrip penghantaran, yang hanyalah skrip shell dengan anotasi khusus Slurm. Anotasi ini membolehkan anda menentukan keperluan sumber, parameter jadual, dan lain-lain. Mari buat skrip shell `hello_world.sh` untuk ini.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Let’s go over the submission file and see what is happening here.\n"
msgstr "Mari tinjau fail penghantaran dan lihat apa yang berlaku di sini.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "`#SBATCH` directives are specific annotations to tell what requirements we have for program execution. Here you can specify the amount of resources - the number of nodes, number of tasks per node, the number of tasks and CPUs per node and task - and other options like the name of the output file. A full list of options is available in the [documentation](https://slurm.schedmd.com/documentation.html) for Slurm.\n"
msgstr "Arahan `#SBATCH` adalah anotasi khusus untuk memberitahu keperluan yang kita ada untuk pelaksanaan program. Di sini anda boleh menentukan jumlah sumber — bilangan nod, bilangan tugas per nod, bilangan tugas dan CPU per nod dan tugas — dan pilihan lain seperti nama fail output. Senarai penuh pilihan tersedia dalam [dokumentasi](https://slurm.schedmd.com/documentation.html) untuk Slurm.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Now it's time to run our Slurm job. `sbatch` is a command that accepts a submission file and enqueues the job for execution in Slurm.\n"
msgstr "Kini sudah tiba masanya untuk menjalankan kerja Slurm kita. `sbatch` ialah arahan yang menerima fail penghantaran dan memasukkan kerja ke dalam giliran untuk pelaksanaan dalam Slurm.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Let's check the status of our program using `squeue` command.\n"
msgstr "Mari semak status program kita menggunakan arahan `squeue`.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Once the job has finished, we can check the result by looking at the output file.\n"
msgstr "Setelah kerja selesai, kita boleh menyemak keputusan dengan melihat fail output.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
